  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations $x={t}^{2}+2,\\,y={t}^{4}+{t}^{2}.$ Given  that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{t}^{2}+1,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}.$",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the second derivative, we can use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{dt})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{t}^{2}+1,$ and we can calculate $\\frac{dx}{dt}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dx}{dt}=\\frac{d}{dt}({t}^{2}+2)=2t\n$$\n\nSubstituting into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{dt}(2{t}^{2}+1)}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{2t} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
